Map IPv6 (NAT66) TCP and UDP the way 200-hardening and 210-tcp map IPv4: the
IPv6 target had the same unbounded, mapping-killing port search with a u16
range that wraps to 0 for --to-ports 0-65535 (a division by zero with
--random), and it was UDP only. Mappings carry their protocol, the search is
capped at 128 probes from a random start and falls back to plain SNAT, and
the IPv6 rule check validates the nf_nat_range layout it actually gets (the
IPv4 check rejected IPv6 rules outright; that family check is gone now that
each family has its own validator). newrange is zeroed: nf_nat_range2 has
fields the rule's nf_nat_range does not.

